This project has AI-assisted insights into 2025 crime statistics recently released by Statistics Canada. 

AI-assisted preparation notes: 
These numbers were generated partially "by hand" in a Colab notebook to allow for human line of sight (beginner-intermediate coder) and AI syntax suggestions. 
Gemini assisted with writing and debugging the Python code. 
Visual spotcheck against Statistics Canada numbers was done, with no errors found.
Notebook checked by Claude Opus 4.7, minor code errors found and fixed. 
Powerpoint deck drafted for review by Claude Opus 4.7. Visual spot-check done and some corrections made to initial version, then spot-checked again. 

Insights:
Statistics Canada has released comprehensive crime statistics for 2025. These show that crime in Canada declined in 2025. The Crime Severity Index, which gives more weight to serious criminal violations, ticked down -4.86 in 2025. 
The overall police-reported crime rate per 100,000 population was also down, by -2.4%. 
Police clearance rates had been generally declining but ticked up slightly in 2025.
Cybercrimes, of which fraud was the most common, had been increasing for a decade until 2023, when they began a slight decrease that continued in 2025. 
2025 also saw a decrease in organized crime and double-digit declines in some serious crimes, such as breaking and entering and homicide. 
The number of police-reported hate crimes decreased 3%.
However, there was a sharp increase in lesser property crime, notably shoplifting under $5,000. 
Sexual assault level 2 and arson were also up.

For visuals, see the executive brief deck. The Colab notebook has more detail. 

Disclaimer: Please do not rely on these for any material purposes and do your own research instead.

